  "webb": {
    "question": "Give me a quick overview of the James Webb Space Telescope.",
    "sentences": [
      {
        "i": 0,
        "text": "The James Webb Space Telescope is an infrared space observatory led by NASA in partnership with ESA and the Canadian Space Agency.",
        "claim": 0.9999985781096752
      },
      {
        "i": 1,
        "text": "It launched on 25 December 2021 aboard a SpaceX Falcon Heavy rocket.",
        "claim": 0.999997078381178
      },
      {
        "i": 2,
        "text": "Webb orbits the Earth at an altitude of about 1.5 million kilometres.",
        "claim": 0.3946240080761867
      },
      {
        "i": 3,
        "text": "Its 6.5-metre primary mirror is built from 18 gold-plated beryllium segments.",
        "claim": 0.9999856428918987
      },
      {
        "i": 4,
        "text": "A five-layer sunshield the size of a tennis court keeps its instruments extremely cold.",
        "claim": 0.9999323950562108
      },
      {
        "i": 5,
        "text": "Webb was the first telescope ever to take a direct photograph of a planet outside our solar system.",
        "claim": 0.9997461105561547
      },
      {
        "i": 6,
        "text": "Its first full-colour images were released in July 2022.",
        "claim": 0.9999603665663568
      },
      {
        "i": 7,
        "text": "The telescope is named after James E. Webb, who ran NASA during the Apollo era.",
        "claim": 0.9999689063838035
      },
      {
        "i": 8,
        "text": "The whole project cost less than 1 billion US dollars.",
        "claim": 0.9999678015863482
      }
    ],
    "passages": [
      {
        "i": 0,
        "doc": "Mission overview",
        "text": "The James Webb Space Telescope (JWST) is a space telescope designed to conduct infrared astronomy. It is an international collaboration led by NASA, with the European Space Agency (ESA) and the Canadian Space Agency (CSA) as partners."
      },
      {
        "i": 1,
        "doc": "Mission overview",
        "text": "The telescope is named after James E. Webb, who served as NASA administrator from 1961 to 1968 during the Mercury, Gemini and Apollo programs."
      },
      {
        "i": 2,
        "doc": "Launch and orbit",
        "text": "JWST launched on 25 December 2021 on an Ariane 5 rocket from the Guiana Space Centre in Kourou, French Guiana."
      },
      {
        "i": 3,
        "doc": "Launch and orbit",
        "text": "Rather than orbiting Earth, Webb orbits the Sun near the second Lagrange point (L2), about 1.5 million kilometres from Earth, where it can keep its sunshield between the telescope and the Sun, Earth and Moon."
      },
      {
        "i": 4,
        "doc": "Design",
        "text": "Its primary mirror is 6.5 metres across and is made of 18 hexagonal segments of gold-plated beryllium, which folded up to fit inside the rocket's fairing."
      },
      {
        "i": 5,
        "doc": "Design",
        "text": "A five-layer sunshield, roughly the size of a tennis court, keeps the instruments at around −233 °C so that the telescope's own heat does not swamp faint infrared signals."
      },
      {
        "i": 6,
        "doc": "Science and cost",
        "text": "Webb's first full-colour images were released in July 2022. Its infrared instruments let it study the earliest galaxies, the formation of stars and planets, and the atmospheres of exoplanets."
      },
      {
        "i": 7,
        "doc": "Science and cost",
        "text": "Including development and early operations, the project's cost is estimated at roughly 10 billion US dollars."
      }
    ],
